--- Short description: ---
[b]Split up your boys in two teams and do a drill battle — on land, at sea (War Sails), or a siege drill on your own castle walls.
Pick your battleground (and time) before the drills — and before the real battles.[/b]


--- Description ---
[size=4][b]Looking for…?[/b][/size]

If one of these searches brought you here — yes, this is that mod:

[list]
[*]a mod for [b]practice battles[/b] / [b]sparring[/b] — train your troops with no losses (deaths near zero, can be turned fully off)
[*]a mod for [b]naval training battles[/b] / practice [b]sea battles[/b] with your own fleet (War Sails)
[*]a mod for [b]siege practice[/b] — storm or defend [b]your own castle walls[/b] in a mock siege
[*]a mod to [b]change the time of day of battles[/b] — fight at [b]full bright daylight[/b], night, dawn, noon or evening
[*]a mod to [b]pick the battlefield[/b] / [b]choose where you fight[/b] before a battle
[*]a mod to [b]scout the battlefield before the battle[/b] — walk it on foot, see the real deployment lines
[*]a mod to [b]preview the deployment[/b] before committing to an attack
[*]a mod to [b]free sail your ship[/b] — take the [b]flagship[/b] out with your crew and scout the sea lanes (War Sails)
[/list]

A mod built on two pillars:

[size=4][b]Pillar 1 — Training battles[/b][/size]

Press [b]G[/b] on the campaign map to open the muster.

[list]
[*][b]Split your army[/b] into two halves through a troop-picking screen, choose which half you command
[*][b]At sea too (War Sails)[/b] — drill while sailing: the fleet divides with the men (proportional to crew, your flagship always stays yours — the muster shows the exact split before you commit), or divide it [b]at your word[/b] through the ship-divide window, sending exactly the hulls you choose opposite. Every hull comes back afterward: sunk, damaged or captured, training costs no ships
[*][b]Pay[/b] for the drill — broken javelins, arrows, rewards, they cost money… (1 daily wage by default, 2 at sea)
[*][b]Choose your side[/b] — attack or defend, one standing choice — then [b]fight[/b] the battle yourself or send the men in and watch it resolve from the hill
[*][b]Level up[/b] your soldiers — keep a share of the XP, scaled by your [b]Quartermaster[/b] (the [b]First Mate[/b] at sea) — a great one even earns bonus XP
[*]The [b]KIA[/b] → most get back up; a few wake up wounded, and in unlucky cases there is a real casualty — a good [b]Surgeon[/b] helps a lot (deaths can be turned fully off)
[*]The [b]wounded[/b] → they get up OK; a few may stay wounded, the surgeon again
[*][b]No loot[/b] — nothing is looted in sparring (works even alongside loot mods like BannerLoot)
[*][b]Disorganized[/b] after — they had to form up etc.
[*][b]Cooldown[/b] — you have to wait before the next drill (24 in-game hours by default)
[/list]

[b]Mock enemy[/b] [i](on by default, can be turned off)[/i] — compose a phantom enemy force from [b]every troop in the game[/b], all cultures in one picker screen, any mix, up to 1000 men — and drill the whole company against it. The phantoms never touch your roster and vanish afterward; your own men follow the normal training rules. The test bench for "how would we fare against X?". At sea the mock enemy [b]sails[/b]: lay down the phantom fleet — any culture's hulls, fitted as well as you please — and its ships vanish with its men.

[b]Castle siege drills[/b] — at your [b]own castle[/b] the muster becomes a [b]siege drill[/b]: storm your own walls or hold them, on the castle's real scene at its true wall level. The [b]garrison and militia[/b] always stand with the defense (take them into your party first if you want them under your own banner), your [b]Engineer[/b] readies the siege equipment for both sides, and everyone on the field follows the same training rules — wounds healed, XP kept, the surgeon watching over the fallen. A grand muster is a public event: it costs more than a field drill, each castle keeps its own clock, and the realm takes note — a little [b]renown and influence[/b] for the show of arms. The mock enemy comes to the walls too: the phantoms besiege you when you hold them, or reinforce the garrison when you storm. The drill waits behind its own option on the castle menu.

[size=4][b]Pillar 2 — Scouting & choosing your ground[/b][/size]

What a real general does before the battle: know the field — and pick the hour.

[list]
[*][b]Choose the time of day[/b] — morning, noon, afternoon, evening, night, or the campaign clock (default). Applies to drills, real field battles, sieges and sea battles alike
[*][b]Select the battlefield[/b] — pick from every battlefield the game could use here: the map patch's own scene (marked [i]"this ground"[/i]) plus every scene of the local terrain type
[*][b]Ride out and scout[/b] — walk any of those battlefields [b]alone[/b]: no battle, no cost, no cooldown. You spawn on your deployment line, facing the enemy's, distance called out
[*][b]Scout the sea too (War Sails)[/b] — afloat, the same option takes your [b]flagship[/b] out instead: you and your crew alone on the chosen water. Sail her, row with the men, learn the sea lane — no battle, no cost, no clock, and the hull comes home without a scratch. A free sail, nothing more — by design
[*][b]Scouted lines = drilled lines[/b] — drills use the same deterministic deployment as the scout, so what you scouted is what you get. In a [i]real[/i] battle the ground holds, but the enemy's true approach direction may turn the field around
[*][b]Same tools in REAL battles[/b] [i](per-side toggles, both on by default)[/i] — the encounter menu offers the same options (hour, scout, battlefield) when defending [b]or[/b] attacking. Both armies stand frozen, so the scouted lines and facings are [b]exact[/b] — and no time passes while you ride
[*][b]Out-scout to earn it[/b] — in a real battle, picking the ground or a non-daylight hour must be earned: your [b]Scout[/b] against the enemy's best — nearly as good when defending, clearly better when attacking (the [b]Navigator[/b] at sea). The campaign clock and full daylight are always free. The lone scout ride is never gated
[*][b]Rule of thumb[/b] — scout from the muster to [i]learn[/i] the battlefields, scout from the attack screen to [i]plan the actual fight[/i]
[/list]

[size=4][b]Your companions matter[/b][/size]

The drill yard runs on your officers — every role you fill makes training better:

[list]
[*][b]Quartermaster[/b] [i](the [b]First Mate[/b] at sea)[/i] — sets the share of XP the men keep from a drill; a great one even earns bonus XP
[*][b]Your skilled fighters[/b] — the best fighter companions instruct on the yard, adding to the XP kept
[*][b]Steward[/b] — a well-run camp: shortens the time it takes to organize the next drill
[*][b]Surgeon[/b] — fewer incidents in training: fewer real deaths among the fallen, fewer men who stay wounded
[*][b]Engineer[/b] — commands the siege drill's arsenal: higher Engineering unlocks heavier siege equipment for the wall drills
[*][b]Scout[/b] [i](the [b]Navigator[/b] at sea)[/i] — wins you the ground: out-scouting the enemy unlocks the battlefield and hour picks in real battles
[/list]

All of it tunable in MCM — each officer has their own group of knobs.

[size=4][b]The knobs[/b][/size]

Every parameter lives in a JSON config ([i]Documents\Mount and Blade II Bannerlord\Configs\TrainingBattles\config.json[/i]) [b]and[/b] in the in-game [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/mountandblade2bannerlord/mods/612]Mod Configuration Menu (MCM)[/url]. MCM is a soft dependency — without it the config file alone runs the show.

[list]
[*][b]Officer knobs[/b] — the Quartermaster's XP band, the fighter-instructor bonus, the Steward cooldown speed-up, the Surgeon's death/wounded bands, the Engineer's equipment tiers, the scouting duel ratios (each officer has its own MCM group)
[*][b]Drill knobs[/b] — hero health restored %, cooldown hours, drill pay (land and sea, days of wages), the castle drill's own pay, clock and prestige rates, disorganized toggle, auto-split-in-half, the opponent half's banner (any banner-editor code)
[*][b]Ground & time knobs[/b] — time of day for battles, the real-battle scout toggles (defending and attacking separately)
[*][b]Misc[/b] — the mock-enemy and castle-drill toggles, the hotkey (default [b]G[/b] on the campaign map), debug logging
[/list]

[size=4][b]The road ahead[/b][/size]

Next milestones, in the order they'll likely land:

[list]
[*][b]Town training sieges[/b] — drill in your own [b]cities[/b]: bigger walls, bigger stakes, the Lord's Hall and all
[*][b]Army training[/b] — drill while leading a hosted [b]army[/b]: march the whole banner against a mock enemy
[/list]

No dates promised — this is a hobby, and playtesting sets the pace.

[size=4][b]Safe & compatible[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]A training battle never silently costs you: no ships lost at sea, no prisoner loss, no loot loss, no relation or crime side effects. The only real cost is the small death chance above — set it to 0 for no deaths at all
[*]War Sails is [b]optional[/b] — without the DLC everything works on land as before
[*]Cancel is always available before the fight starts and leaves the campaign exactly as it was
[*]No Harmony patches — the mod rides vanilla's own model chain, so it stacks politely with other mods
[*]Safe to add to an existing save; when removing, finish (or cancel) any running drill first
[/list]

[size=4][b]Installation[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Extract the archive into your game's [i]Modules[/i] folder (so you get [i]Modules\TrainingBattles\...[/i]), or install with Vortex
[*]Enable [b]Training Battles[/b] in the launcher — load it after the official modules (and after MCM / War Sails if you use them; the manifest declares the order, the launcher sorts it for you)
[*]Also on the [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3770681619]Steam Workshop[/url]
[/list]

